1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,500
The Incredible Octopus.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:16,800
Octopuses (order Octopoda, class Cephalopoda) are among the most neurologically complex invertebrates, with approximately 300 species distributed from shallow coral reefs to abyssal plains exceeding 5,000 meters depth.

3
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:32,812
The common octopus (Octopus vulgaris) possesses roughly 500 million neurons — comparable in number to a dog — with two-thirds distributed in eight semi-autonomous arm ganglia, allowing each arm to process tactile and chemosensory information independently.

4
00:00:32,812 --> 00:00:38,050
Cognition research has demonstrated sophisticated problem-solving capabilities.

5
00:00:38,050 --> 00:00:48,575
In laboratory settings, octopuses have opened childproof containers, learned to distinguish visual patterns through operant conditioning, and navigated complex maze tasks.

6
00:00:48,575 --> 00:01:03,787
Research at the University of Chicago documented individual octopuses using coconut shell halves as portable shelters, while studies in Indonesia observed veined octopuses (Amphioctopus marginatus) carrying and assembling coconut halves as dens.

7
00:01:03,787 --> 00:01:07,937
Maze experiments show memory retention exceeding two weeks.

8
00:01:07,937 --> 00:01:17,662
Octopuses display distinct individual temperaments: some are bold explorers, others cautious, consistent with personality traits observed across vertebrates.

9
00:01:17,662 --> 00:01:22,075
Camouflage is mediated by three specialized dermal cell layers.

10
00:01:22,075 --> 00:01:30,475
Chromatophores (sac-like pigment cells innervated by nerves) expand or contract within milliseconds, producing color changes.

11
00:01:30,475 --> 00:01:38,025
Below these, iridophores reflect structural color through thin-film interference, creating iridescent blues and greens.

12
00:01:38,025 --> 00:01:44,825
The deepest layer, leucophores, scatter ambient light, producing white and enabling background matching.

13
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:58,875
The mimic octopus (Thaumoctopus mimicus) can impersonate up to 15 marine species, including lionfish, sea snakes, and flatfish, by altering body shape, posture, and swimming pattern simultaneously.

14
00:01:58,875 --> 00:02:03,850
Most octopus species are semelparous — reproducing once before death.

15
00:02:03,850 --> 00:02:12,975
Females lay 20,000 to 200,000 eggs, depending on species, and guard them continuously for 1 to 10 months without feeding.

16
00:02:12,975 --> 00:02:17,862
Shortly after the eggs hatch, the female dies from programmed senescence.

17
00:02:17,862 --> 00:02:26,050
Lifespan is typically 1 to 3 years, though the giant Pacific octopus (Enteroctopus dofleini) can reach 5 years.

18
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:37,787
The blood uses copper-based hemocyanin rather than iron-based hemoglobin, appearing blue and providing efficient oxygen transport in cold, low-oxygen environments down to 2 degrees Celsius.

